Notes for review:

* `attacks_bb` above is spelled `attackers_bb` in the source; the pre-filter it
  comes from is unchanged.
* The candidate loop keeps the original's cap of eight and the original's
  promotion equality (the candidate is built with `mv.promotion()`).
* `make_move_unchecked` is safe here because the pre-filter is exact
  pseudo-legality and pawns never reach this branch (a pawn capture is
  disambiguated by its file). The function's existing contract already requires
  the caller to keep the `checkers` cache current, which `make_move_unchecked`
  does and `unmake_move` undoes - the same bookkeeping the movegen performed.
* `us.other()` is the side to move after the candidate's move, i.e. the attacker
  colour. The two values are the whole correctness of this hunk, and getting
  them wrong is easy in a way that is invisible on a casual test. An earlier
  attempt at this study asked
  `tmp.attackers_to(tmp.king_square(tmp.turn()).0, tmp.turn(), tmp.occupied())` -
  the king of the side to move *after* the candidate, attacked by that same
  side's pieces. That question has no meaning, and on the first position the
  study hit it answered "attacked":

      fen  rn1qkb1r/pQp2ppp/4pn2/8/2B5/2N4P/PPPP1PP1/R1B1K2R b KQkq - 0 8
      candidate  Nf6-d7  (the alternative to the move actually played, Nb8-d7)
      gigachess   Nbd7            <- the hint is right: Nf6-d7 is legal
      wrong form  Nd7             <- the candidate was judged illegal
      the "attack" that decided it: White's own rook on h1 defends its own
      king on e1, so `attackers_to(e1, White, ...)` is non-empty

  The failure is one-directional - it *drops* hints that should be there, so the
  output is under-disambiguated (measured: 83,337 of 419,385 games exact against
  407,350, all 335,990 extra differences of the over-disambiguation kind) and
  never invents one. Two things keep it honest: the values above are taken from
  `board` (the caller's position, before the candidate is made) rather than from
  the mutated copy, and the gold comparison is the gate - one run over the
  419,385 games of the reference corpus, which reproduces 407,350 exactly with
  this hunk in place.
* No public API changes, no new types, no behaviour change for any other caller
  of `move_to_san_body` / `move_to_san`.
